Camera snapshots through Fabric
================================

The cameras.snapshot Fabric operation now resolves its stored actor from the
current database state before asking StreamService to capture a frame. It requires
an active, undeleted author in the invocation organization, the cameras.view
permission at the camera's site, and the camera/group live-access grant enforced
by the normal camera API. The camera itself must be undeleted and belong to that
organization. Missing identity, unknown targets and failed authorization lookups
refuse before credentials are decrypted or a snapshot connection is opened.

Current author grants cannot widen an explicitly narrower accessible_site_ids
supplied by the invocation context. An empty explicit set denies site-bound
cameras. Existing hybrid behavior for authors with no grants and org-level
resources with no site is unchanged, as are the administrator rules implemented
by the shared CurrentUser and camera-access services.

Successful snapshots still return their image/jpeg artifact, size and hash.
Authorization refusals and capture failures use fixed messages, avoiding raw
client exceptions that could contain device URLs or credentials.

This covers the Fabric snapshot handler, separately from native automation camera
actions. It does not add transaction-wide authorization fencing, revoke a frame
already captured, change camera group policy, or claim that other Fabric handlers
have all been qualified. No device write operation is introduced.
